<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Antiochus IV Epiphanes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 215–164 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Antiochus IV</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Antiochus IV Epiphanes was a Seleucid king who reigned from 175 to 164 BCE. He invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> but was forced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> to withdraw. His attempts to impose Hellenistic religious practice in Judea provoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maccabean-revolt">Maccabean Revolt</a>, which contributed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a>'s loss of territory and is commemorated in the festival of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanukkah">Hanukkah</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antiochus-iv-epiphanes.jpg" alt="Antiochus IV Epiphanes" width="280" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: José Luiz · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Antiochus_IV_Epiphanes_-_Altes_Museum_-_Berlin_-_Germany_2017.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Antiochus was a son of the Seleucid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III the Great</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-apamea">Treaty of Apamea</a> of 188 BCE he became a political hostage of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-republic">Roman Republic</a>, and when his elder brother Seleucus IV Philopator took the throne in 187 BCE he was exchanged for Seleucus's son Demetrius. He then lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, where he was when Seleucus was assassinated in 175 BCE by the minister Heliodorus. With the help of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eumenes-ii">Eumenes II</a> of Pergamum he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> by November 175 BCE and took the throne as co-regent with Seleucus's infant son, who died in 170 BCE, possibly murdered by Antiochus.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Antiochus cultivated a reputation for extravagance. He scattered money in the streets of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and held a great parade at Daphne, and his unexpected behaviour, such as appearing in the public baths, led detractors to call him Epimanes, the Mad, a play on his title Epiphanes, God Manifest. In 170 BCE the guardians of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-vi-philometor">Ptolemy VI Philometor</a> declared war to recover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coele-syria">Coele-Syria</a>. Antiochus defeated the Egyptian army, advanced into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and captured Ptolemy. In 168 BCE he attacked again, but before he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> the Roman envoy Gaius Popillius Laenas delivered a Senate order to withdraw, drew a circle in the sand round him, and demanded an answer before he left it. Antiochus withdrew.</p><p>In 171 BCE Antiochus had replaced the High Priest Jason with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelaus">Menelaus</a>, who offered a bribe. In 168 BCE a rumour that the king had died led Jason to attack <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and Antiochus, returning, restored Menelaus and killed many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. According to 1 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maccabees">Maccabees</a> he then outlawed Jewish rites, made the Temple a cult that included the worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a>, and built a Greek citadel, the Acra. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maccabean-revolt">Maccabean Revolt</a> followed. Traditionally it is seen as resistance to foreign oppression, but some modern scholars see Antiochus as intervening in a civil war between traditionalist and Hellenized Jews.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Antiochus led the main army east against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parthian-empire">Parthians</a>, retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, but was driven from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a>, and died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a> in 164 BCE. Polybius and the books of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maccabees">Maccabees</a> judge him harshly, but some historians doubt Polybius, who was a friend of Antiochus's rival Demetrius I. Jewish tradition remembers him as the persecutor of the story of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanukkah">Hanukkah</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Antiochus IV Epiphanes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Rome steadily extended its influence over Egyptian affairs: in 168 BCE a Roman envoy forced <b>Antiochus IV</b> to abandon his invasion of Egypt, and in 55 BCE Roman troops restored Ptolemy XII to his throne.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ptolemaic-empire">Ptolemaic Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…traditional laws and internal government: Babylonian temple communities continued to operate under cuneiform legal traditions, the Jews of Judaea retained their ancestral law (until <b>Antiochus IV</b>'s intervention), and Greek poleis kept their civic institutions, while a layer of royal authority above these arrangements extracted revenue and military service.</blockquote><a href="/empire/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Hanukkah, the eight-day festival of Judaism that begins on 25 Kislev, usually in December, commemorates an event: the rededication of the Temple in Jerusalem in 164 BCE by Judas Maccabeus and his followers, after the Seleucid king <b>Antiochus IV Epiphanes</b> had desecrated it.</blockquote><a href="/essays/midwinter">Midwinter</a></li><li><blockquote>In 167 BCE <b>Antiochus IV</b> of the Seleucid Empire banned Jewish practices, provoking a revolt led by the priest Mattathias and his sons, the Maccabees.</blockquote><a href="/state/hasmonean-kingdom">Hasmonean Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Rome annexed Commagene in 17 CE, restored it to <b>Antiochus IV</b> in 38 CE, and finally absorbed it into the province of Syria in 72 CE under Vespasian.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-commagene">Kingdom of Commagene</a></li></ul></section>
